Context()->variables->add(y => 'Real');
$y = -5;
$f = Compute("-2(4) + 3");
$truefalse = DropDown(['The equation is true.','The equation is fase.'], 0, showInStatic => 0);

######
# Body
######

BEGIN_PGML
The point [`(4,-5)`] is on the graph in Figure[$NBSP]*3.2.3(c). What happens when you substitute these values into the equation [`y=-2x+3\text{?}`]

>> [@DataTable(
  [
    [
     [PGML('[`y`]'),
      rowbottom => '2',
      midrule => '1',],
     PGML('[`=`]'),
     PGML('[`-2x+3`]'),
    ],
    [
     PGML('[_]{$y}{2}'),
     PGML('[`\wonder{=}`]'),
     PGML('[`-2(`][_]{4}{2}[`)+3`]'),
    ],
    [
     PGML('[_]{$y}{2}'),
     PGML('[`\wonder{=}`]'),
     PGML('[_]{$f}{10}'),
    ],
  ],
      align => 'rcl',
      valign => 'middle',);@]* <<

[_]{$truefalse}{5}

END_PGML

##########
# Solution
##########

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Each side of the equation simplifies to [`-5\text{.}`] In other words, the equation simplifies to an outright true equation.

END_PGML_SOLUTION

